<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kars</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kars is a city in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, on a high plateau near the Armenian border, dominated by a medieval citadel. It was the capital of Bagratid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in the tenth century and then of the small Kingdom of Kars, and later a contested Ottoman fortress, held by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> from 1878 to 1918. The Treaty of Kars of 1921 confirmed it as part of Turkey.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kars.jpg" alt="Kars" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ben Men Lyun · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Holy_Apostles_Church_of_Kars_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1740 he campaigned in Central Asia, subduing Bukhara and Khiva, while maintaining military pressure on the Ottomans in the west, culminating in his victory at <b>Kars</b> in 1745.</blockquote><a href="/empire/afsharid-empire">Afsharid Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Republic of Armenia, declared on 28 May 1918, was invaded in 1920 by Turkish nationalist forces and by the 11th Red Army, which entered Yerevan in early December; the Treaty of <b>Kars</b> then ceded Kars, Ardahan and Surmalu, with Mount Ararat, to Turkey.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Georgia is a transit corridor between Europe and Asia bypassing Russia, through Batumi and Poti, the Baku–Tbilisi–Ceyhan pipeline and the Baku–Tbilisi–<b>Kars</b> railway opened in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/empire/georgia">Georgia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>From the 14th century the Yazidi lands formed seven centres, each with its sinjaq: Lalish and Sheikhan, Shingal (Sinjar), Hakkari, the lands around Siirt and Mardin, Aleppo and Afrin, Tabriz, and Serhed around <b>Kars</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/yazidism">Yazidism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Ottoman defeat it expanded to <b>Kars</b>, and the Treaty of Sevres in 1920 promised it much of eastern Anatolia.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-republic-of-armenia">First Republic of Armenia</a></li><li><blockquote>It rejected the Treaty of Sevres, signed treaties with Soviet Russia at Moscow and <b>Kars</b>, and secured French withdrawal by the Treaty of Ankara.</blockquote><a href="/state/government-of-the-grand-national-assembly">Government of the Grand National Assembly</a></li></ul></section>
